<script>
import { url } from '@abide/abide'
import getRegistrationSource from 'crm/src/companies/getRegistration.handler.ts' with { type: 'text' }
import Registration from 'crm/src/companies/lib/Registration.abide'
import registrationSource from 'crm/src/companies/lib/Registration.abide' with { type: 'text' }
import registrationTestSource from 'crm/src/companies/lib/Registration.test.ts' with { type: 'text' }

import Doc from '#src/lib/Doc.abide'
import Example from '#src/lib/Example.abide'
import Registry from '#src/lib/Registry.abide'
import Section from '#src/lib/Section.abide'
</script>

<Doc>
    <Section title="`fetchJson` asks a failing service again, and a lasting failure is a refusal">
        <Example
            id="fetch-json"
            title={"`fetchJson(url, { ttl })` — a company's entry at the registry"}
            summary="The CRM fills in a company from the business registry, which is having a bad day. Hopper & Co answers on the third ask; Langley Research never does."
            serverFiles={['src/companies/lib/registryOrigin.server.ts']}
            files={{
                'src/companies/lib/Registration.abide': registrationSource,
                'src/companies/getRegistration.handler.ts': getRegistrationSource,
                'src/companies/lib/Registration.test.ts': registrationTestSource,
            }}
        >
            <Registration />
        </Example>

        <p>The handler returns what <code>fetchJson</code> settles to, and does nothing else. A
            body is the answer. A service that gave up is a <code>FetchFailed</code>, a refusal at
            502, and returning one refuses, so the page reads it from <code>error()</code> with its
            message. See <a href={url('/reactive/refusals')}>Refusals</a>. Nothing is thrown but
            your own abort: pass the request's <code>signal</code>, and a caller who leaves stops
            the asking.</p>

        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>An attempt that</th><th>Is</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td>answers 2xx</td><td>the body</td></tr>
                <tr><td>throws, or runs past <code>timeout</code> (10 s)</td><td>asked again</td></tr>
                <tr><td>answers 408, 425, 429 or 5xx</td><td>asked again, after its <code>Retry-After</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td>answers any other status</td><td>a <code>FetchFailed</code> at once</td></tr>
                <tr><td>is the last of <code>retries</code> (2)</td><td>a <code>FetchFailed</code> carrying its status</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>The waits double from 250 ms, and a <code>Retry-After</code> takes their place. One
            asking for longer than <code>timeout</code> is not waited on: a service saying come back
            in an hour is a failure now. A <code>POST</code> is asked once unless you set
            <code>retries</code>, since asking it again may write twice. A retry sends the body
            again, so give one as a string rather than a stream.</p>

        <p><code>ttl</code> holds a <code>GET</code>'s answer in memory for that many milliseconds,
            keyed by its URL and its headers, so two members' credentials are two answers. Two asks
            at once are one request, which the test counts. A failure is never held. The cache is
            this process's: a restart empties it, and a second server keeps its own.</p>

        <p>Give a <code>schema</code> and the body is checked before it is the answer, a body it
            refuses being a <code>FetchFailed</code> too. Without one, the type you name is a
            promise about the service nothing checks. See <a href={url('/transports/schemas')}>Schemas</a>.</p>
    </Section>

    <Registry />
</Doc>
